Condition.template:
Using only the retrieved PostgreSQL schema metadata, select one best clinical relation for diagnoses or conditions in any of these source schemas: {source_schemas}, plus at most one matching dictionary relation when its join columns are documented. Do not combine procedures, billing events, or multiple condition sources. Generate a SELECT filtered to patient key {patient_key} and selected encounter keys in ({encounter_keys}). The selected encounter lineage is supplied below. It is runtime evidence from the executed SQL, not a hospital mapping. Do not assume that the clinical relation stores an identically named or semantically identical encounter column; use documented PK/FK or other schema-supported joins back to the selected Encounter relation when needed.
{encounter_context}
{relationship_retry_instruction}
Return: patient key AS _patient_key, the selected canonical encounter key AS _encounter_key, a non-null unique stable condition-row key built from documented columns AS _resource_key, code AS _code, canonical code-system URI AS _code_system only when stored in a source column otherwise NULL, source description from an actually joined documented dictionary column AS _display otherwise NULL. Every expression must use documented columns or NULL. Never invent values, descriptions, systems, or placeholder text. Order deterministically and limit {limit}. Fully qualify every relation.

Procedure.template:
Using only the retrieved PostgreSQL schema metadata, select one best clinical relation for patient procedures in any of these source schemas: {source_schemas}, plus at most one matching dictionary relation when documented. Do not combine diagnoses, billing events, or multiple procedure sources. Generate a SELECT filtered to patient key {patient_key} and selected encounter keys in ({encounter_keys}). The selected encounter lineage is supplied below. It is runtime evidence from the executed SQL, not a hospital mapping. Do not assume that the clinical relation stores an identically named or semantically identical encounter column; use documented PK/FK or other schema-supported joins back to the selected Encounter relation when needed.
{encounter_context}
{relationship_retry_instruction}
Return: patient key AS _patient_key, the selected canonical encounter key AS _encounter_key, a non-null unique key built only from that procedure row's documented key columns AS _resource_key, procedure code AS _code, an actual stored canonical URI column AS _code_system otherwise NULL, an actual dictionary description column AS _display otherwise NULL, procedure date/time AS _event_datetime. Every expression must use documented columns or NULL; quoted clinical labels and fallback literals are forbidden. Order deterministically and limit {limit}. Fully qualify every relation.

Observation.template:
Using only the retrieved PostgreSQL schema metadata, select one best event relation for observations or laboratory results in any of these source schemas: {source_schemas}, plus at most one matching dictionary relation. The selected event relation MUST contain an actual documented test, item, measurement, or observation code column; select that source value AS _code and never return NULL for _code. If a relation has no code column, choose another relation. Generate a SELECT filtered to patient key {patient_key} and selected encounter keys in ({encounter_keys}). The selected encounter lineage is supplied below. It is runtime evidence from the executed SQL, not a hospital mapping. Do not assume that the event relation stores an identically named or semantically identical encounter column; use documented PK/FK or other schema-supported joins back to the selected Encounter relation when needed.
{encounter_context}
{relationship_retry_instruction}
Return: patient key AS _patient_key, the selected canonical encounter key AS _encounter_key, an event-row identifier AS _resource_key that is non-null and unique across every returned row. Never use a patient, encounter, visit, or stay key alone as _resource_key; use a documented event-row key or concatenate enough documented event columns to distinguish rows. Also return the required source observation code AS _code, canonical code-system URI AS _code_system when stored otherwise NULL, source display AS _display, event date/time AS _event_datetime, numeric value AS _value_number, text value AS _value_text, display unit AS _unit, canonical unit-system URI AS _unit_system only when stored otherwise NULL, canonical unit code AS _unit_code only when stored otherwise NULL, reference low AS _reference_low, reference high AS _reference_high, interpretation AS _interpretation. Never invent or normalize unsupported source values. Order deterministically and limit {limit}. Fully qualify every relation.
